[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m [response] To solve the problem, we start by determining the side length of the original square. Given that the area of the square is \(49\) square inches, we can find the side length by taking the square root of the area:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m s = \sqrt{49} = 7 \text{ inches}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m When the square is folded so that one pair of opposite corners coincide, a diagonal is formed. This diagonal divides the square into two congruent right triangles. Each triangle has legs that are equal to the side length of the square, and the hypotenuse is the diagonal of the square.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The length of the diagonal \(d\) of a square with side length \(s\) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m d = \sqrt{s^2 + s^2} = \sqrt{7^2 + 7^2} = \sqrt{49 + 49} = \sqrt{98} = 7\sqrt{2} \text{ inches}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Each of the two congruent triangles has legs of \(7\) inches and a hypotenuse of \(7\sqrt{2}\) inches. To find the perimeter of one of these triangles, we sum the lengths of its sides:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \text{Perimeter} = 7 + 7 + 7\sqrt{2} = 14 + 7\sqrt{2}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Thus, the perimeter of one of the triangles is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \boxed{14 + 7\sqrt{2}}
