[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we can first calculate the total number of ways to choose 3 marbles out of 11, which is given by the combination formula \(C(n, k) = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\), where \(n\) is the total number of items, \(k\) is the number of items to choose, and \(!\) denotes factorial. So, the total number of ways to choose 3 marbles out of 11 is \(C(11, 3) = \frac{11!}{3!(11-3)!} = \frac{11 \times 10 \times 9}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 165\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Next, we calculate the number of ways in which the green and purple marbles are chosen together. If Liz chooses both the green and purple marbles, she has to choose 1 more marble from the remaining 9, which can be done in \(C(9, 1) = 9\) ways.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Therefore, the number of ways Liz can choose 3 marbles without choosing the green and purple ones together is \(165 - 9 = 156\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The final answer is \boxed{156}.
