[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we start by using the relationship between the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of two numbers, which is given by:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ \text{GCD}(m, n) \times \text{LCM}(m, n) = m \times n \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Given that the GCD of \(m\) and \(n\) is 8 and the LCM is 112, we have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 8 \times 112 = m \times n \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 896 = m \times n \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Since the GCD of \(m\) and \(n\) is 8, both \(m\) and \(n\) must be multiples of 8. Let \(m = 8a\) and \(n = 8b\), where \(a\) and \(b\) are coprime (i.e., their GCD is 1). Substituting these into the equation gives:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 896 = 8a \times 8b \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 14 = a \times b \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The pairs \((a, b)\) that satisfy this equation and are coprime are \((1, 14)\) and \((2, 7)\). The pair \((2, 7)\) gives the smallest sum for \(m\) and \(n\):
