[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we need to use the relationship between the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of two numbers. Specifically, for any two positive integers \( m \) and \( n \), the product of the numbers is equal to the product of their GCD and LCM:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ m \times n = \text{GCD}(m, n) \times \text{LCM}(m, n) \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Given that the GCD of \( m \) and \( n \) is 8 and their LCM is 112, we can substitute these values into the equation:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ m \times n = 8 \times 112 = 896 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Since the GCD of \( m \) and \( n \) is 8, both \( m \) and \( n \) must be multiples of 8. Let \( m = 8a \) and \( n = 8b \), where \( a \) and \( b \) are coprime (i.e., their GCD is 1). Substituting these into the equation gives:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 8a \times 8b = 896 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 64ab = 896 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ ab = 14 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The pairs \((a, b)\) that are coprime and multiply to 14 are \((1, 14)\) and \((2, 7)\). The pair \((2, 7)\) will give the smallest sum for \( m \) and \( n \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ m = 8 \times 2 = 16 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ n = 8 \times 7 = 56 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Thus, the least possible value of \( m + n \) is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 16 + 56 = \boxed{72} \]
