[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m [response] To solve the problem, we start by using the relationship between the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of two numbers. Specifically, for any two positive integers \( m \) and \( n \), the product of the numbers is equal to the product of their GCD and LCM:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ m \times n = \text{GCD}(m, n) \times \text{LCM}(m, n) \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Given that \(\text{GCD}(m, n) = 8\) and \(\text{LCM}(m, n) = 112\), we can substitute these values into the equation:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ m \times n = 8 \times 112 = 896 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Since the GCD of \( m \) and \( n \) is 8, we can express \( m \) and \( n \) as \( m = 8a \) and \( n = 8b \), where \( a \) and \( b \) are coprime integers (i. e., \(\text{GCD}(a, b) = 1\)). Substituting these into the product equation gives:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 8a \times 8b = 896 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 64ab = 896 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ ab = 14 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The pairs \((a, b)\) that are coprime and multiply to 14 are \((1, 14)\) and \((2, 7)\). To minimize \( m + n \), we choose the pair \((2, 7)\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ m = 8 \times 2 = 16 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ n = 8 \times 7 = 56 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Thus, the least possible value of \( m + n \) is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ m + n = 16 + 56 = 72 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Therefore, the answer is \(\boxed{72}\).
