[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m [response] To determine the number of vertical asymptotes for the function \( y = \frac{2}{x^2 + x - 6} \), we need to factor the denominator and find the values of \( x \) that make it zero.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The denominator factors as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ x^2 + x - 6 = (x + 3)(x - 2) \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Setting each factor equal to zero gives:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ x + 3 = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad x - 2 = 0 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ x = -3 \quad \text{and} \quad x = 2 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Since these values do not cancel out with any factors in the numerator, the function has vertical asymptotes at \( x = -3 \) and \( x = 2 \). Therefore, the number of vertical asymptotes is \(\boxed{2}\).
