[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m [response] To determine how many three-digit numbers are divisible by 13, we first identify the smallest and largest three-digit multiples of 13.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The smallest three-digit number is 100. When we divide 100 by 13, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 100 \div 13 \approx 7.69 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Rounding up, the smallest integer is 8. So, the smallest three-digit multiple of 13 is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 13 \times 8 = 104 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The largest three-digit number is 999. When we divide 999 by 13, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 999 \div 13 \approx 76.84 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Rounding down, the largest integer is 76. So, the largest three-digit multiple of 13 is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 13 \times 76 = 988 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The three-digit multiples of 13 form an arithmetic sequence:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 104, 117, 130, \ldots, 988 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The number of terms in this sequence can be found using the formula for the \(n\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m where \(a_n = 988\), \(a_1 = 104\), and \(d = 13\). Solving for \(n\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 988 = 104 + (n-1) \cdot 13 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ 884 = (n-1) \cdot 13 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ n-1 = 68 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ n = 69 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Thus, the number of three-digit numbers divisible by 13 is \(\boxed{69}\).
