[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m [response] To determine the number of vertical asymptotes for the function \( y = \frac{2}{x^2 + x - 6} \), we need to find the values of \( x \) that make the denominator zero, as these are the points where the function may have vertical asymptotes.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m The denominator is the quadratic expression \( x^2 + x - 6 \). We can factor this expression as follows:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ x^2 + x - 6 = (x + 3)(x - 2) \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m Setting the factored form equal to zero gives us the roots:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ (x + 3)(x - 2) = 0 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m \[ x = -3 \quad \text{or} \quad x = 2 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2111625)[0m These roots indicate that there are vertical asymptotes at \( x = -3 \) and \( x = 2 \). Therefore, the graph of the function has \(\boxed{2}\) vertical asymptotes.
